From e21101ed7da01335a1f1541041e56f6d3af439b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Beany <24502088+beanyforever@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 07:46:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] The attribute caches keep the newest attributes

SiYuan caches pages' and blocks' attributes in ristretto, which takes in a
key it doesn't hold yet later, on its own goroutine, and turns away a second
put of that key queued before the first is taken in. "+ New" in a database
does this to the page it makes: placing the row renders the view, which
reads the new page's attributes from its file and caches them before the
page is bound (getNearItem, BatchGetBlockAttrs, getBlockAttrsFromTree), and
a fraction of a millisecond later bindBlockAv0 puts them again with
custom-avs. About 1 time in 6 that second put was turned away, and
/api/attr/getBlockAttrs and /api/av/getAttributeViewKeys named no database
for the page until SiYuan restarted. A read that missed the cache also
stored what it had loaded from the file even if a write landed meanwhile, so
it could replace newer attributes with older ones; and a put ristretto
turned away for want of room left the block's other key, the bare one an
edit writes, to be served instead, older still.

Every put to the block or the page attribute cache is now a write or a fill.
A write, for attributes being set, takes the cache's lock, bumps the block's
or the page's version, puts, waits until the cache has taken the put in or
turned it away, and reads it back; one turned away, or too big, removes
every key of the block or page, so reads go to the file rather than to an
older copy. A fill, for what a read that missed loaded from the file, takes
a FillToken before its first lookup and lands only if the key is still
missing and nothing wrote or removed it since. A block's fill also checks
the page it read the block from, since a page write changes its blocks'
attributes without bumping their versions: WriteTree and
WriteTreeIfUnchanged bracket their file write with BeginTreeWrite and
EndTreeWrite, and removing a page from the page cache bumps its version too.
Fills wait like writes, so none is left queued to turn a write away. Removes
and clears take the lock and bump the versions, and the index of each
block's keys is kept under the same lock, so a remove never misses a put.
The lock covers only the cache's own calls, never a file or page load; a
block's fill takes the page cache's lock inside it. Versions outlive removal
and are forgotten all at once past 16,384 keys per cache, refusing the fills
under way.

The fills are getBlockAttrsFromTree, reached from GetBlockAttrs and
BatchGetBlockAttrs, box.docIAL, the index rebuild, indexBoxDocIfNeeded and
an import's reload; every other put is a write. The kanban and gallery
renders hand BatchGetBlockAttrsWitTrees trees they loaded earlier, so it no
longer caches what it reads. Three removes of a page's attributes just
before putting them become one put each, and SetBlockReminder caches the
attributes of the block it set the reminder on, as written, rather than the
copy it read through the cache.

Left open: a write whose entry is gone (evicted, or turned away) before a
fill inside the same transaction, which then reads the file from before the
commit; a failed transaction's puts, which stay cached ahead of the file;
the copies an import or a database duplicate can cache of custom-avs that
the file never gets; and a block's two keys, so a notebook-key entry can
hide a newer edit's bare copy, and a page's updated can lag its edits.

Its Go tests are TestIALCacheKeepsLatestPut and
TestIALCacheTurnedAwayWriteLeavesNoOlderCopy in ial_latest_test.go and the
fill tests in ial_fill_test.go, in kernel/cache;
TestBlockAttrsFillThenWriteKeepsWrite,
TestBatchGetBlockAttrsWitTreesCachesNothing,
TestBlockAttrsFillAfterWriteDoesNotStore and
TestBlockAttrsReadsFillWhatTheyLoad in kernel/sql; and
TestWriteTreeRefusesBlockFillsTakenBefore in kernel/filesys. On v3.8.7 with
0001 to 0003 both tests in ial_latest_test.go fail, and with the first form
of this patch, which only waited for puts, the second does; the others use
the interface this patch adds.
